## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to craft a robust online ICA chain that gracefully handles dynamic sensor availability in a real-world, latency-constrained setting.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic active-channel management (4–6)
- Forgetting-factor whitening for nonstationarity
- Strategy switching: per-bin ICA vs joint diagonalization
- Dropout imputation via subspace projection
- Cross-bin permutation maintenance under channel changes
- Low-latency real-time constraints and evaluation plan

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: frame processing
function processFrame(frame) {
  const active = detectActiveMics(frame);
  const W = whitening(frame, forgettingFactor);
  const strategy = chooseStrategy(active, sparsity(frame));
  const demixed = demix(frame, W, strategy);
  alignPermutation(demixed);
  return demixed;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you estimate sparsity for strategy selection in real time?
- What failure modes arise with burst dropouts and how would you mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design robust distributed signal processing algorithms that can operate under real-world constraints such as asynchrony, packet loss, and computational limitations across edge devices.

## Key Concepts
- Distributed whitening and demixing across multiple nodes
- Gossip-based consensus for matrix fusion
- Time-varying mixing matrix tracking with adaptive algorithms
- Partial observation handling and permutation alignment
- Real-time performance evaluation using SDR/SIR metrics and latency constraints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch for a single gossip round
function gossipRound(W_local, neighbors) {
  const W_agg = average([W_local, ...neighbors.map(n => n.W_local)]);
  return normalize(W_agg);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical, beginner-friendly ICA design that leverages a real-world calibration signal to stabilize whitening and demixing—a common technique when channel responses drift in practice.

## Key Concepts

- Convolutive ICA with short-time framing
- Calibration-based whitening initialization
- Per-bin ICA vs. joint diagonalization trade-offs
- Cross-bin permutation alignment using known chirp timing
- Ground-truth evaluation plan excluding calibration data

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo steps for chirp-informed whitening
// 1) Estimate mixing matrix from chirp cross-correlations
const mixingMatrix = estimateMixingFromChirp(chirpSignals, micPairs);

// 2) Initialize whitening transform per frequency bin
const whiteningMatrix = initializeWhitening(mixingMatrix, freqBins);

// 3) Apply per-bin FastICA with Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization
const demixingMatrices = runFastICA(whitenedSignals, nSources = 2);

// 4) Anchor cross-bin permutation to chirp timing
const itdIldReference = computeITD_ILD(chirpSegment);
alignPermutations(demixingMatrices, itdIldReference);

// 5) Monitor condition number for source-count drift
if (conditionNumber(demixingMatrices[bin]) > 50) {
  reinitializeDemixing(mixingMatrix);
}
```

